{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788286244,"nonce":"0qXhOFEeAbOFlT0DPgp5sMe47nc=","title":"construction cannot be asked about a layout, and silently ignored --layout_id while reporting success","message":"Found by the M2 rehearsal (session 18), while trying to answer the most obvious\nquestion a caller has after `place-layout` returns: *how is my room coming\nalong?*\n\n## What happened\n\n    rwa place-layout templates/bedroom.ir.json --origin 119,126 ...\n    -\u003e layout_id \"ly-1\", placed_count 22\n\n    rwa construction --layout_id ly-1\n    -\u003e {\"rect\":[0,0,250,250], \"rect_source\":\"whole-map\", \"blueprints\":22, ...}\n\nThe answer was right only because those 22 were the only blueprints on the map.\nAsk it on a colony with two layouts in flight and it silently answers about both.\n\n## Two defects, and the second is the one that bites\n\n**1. `construction` has no layout scope.** `ConstructionVerbs.Construction`\n(`:92`) branches on `placement_id`, then `id`, then `rect`, else whole-map.\n`place-layout` MINTS an `ly-N`; `cancel-layout` TAKES an `ly-N`; the verb whose\nwhole job is reporting build progress cannot be asked about one. So a layout can\nbe created and destroyed by id but not observed by id, and the caller is left\neither polling 22 `placement_id`s one at a time or passing the layout's `rect` —\nwhich is not the same set, since a rect catches anything else standing on that\nground.\n\n**2. `--layout_id` was silently ignored and the call reported success.** This is\n`7382bdd`'s open class landing on a new verb, and it is worse here than in the\ncases that issue banks: the fallback is not a harmless default but a DIFFERENT\nQUESTION (whole-map), answered confidently. `rect_source: \"whole-map\"` is in the\nenvelope and is the only tell, and it reads as informational rather than as \"your\nargument went in the bin\". A caller who trusted the number would have been right\ntoday by luck and wrong tomorrow.\n\nFiled separately from `7382bdd` because fixing (1) does not fix (2): a\n`layout_id` branch would make THIS arg work while every other typo on this verb\nstays silent, and refusing unknown args on `construction` alone does not give the\nlayout a scope. `7382bdd` remains the general question and stays open on its own\nterms; this issue is the concrete pair `place-layout` created.\n\n## Why it matters beyond convenience\n\n`fc287ba` (1.6, `advance until:condition`) wants to halt on a state predicate,\nand \"this layout is finished\" is the predicate M2 actually needed — see the\ncomment added there. A predicate can only address what an observer can scope, so\na layout-scoped read is the prerequisite for a layout-completion halt, not a\nnicety beside it.\n\n## Scope\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/ConstructionVerbs.cs`. `Placements` already indexes by layout\nand `Placements.Answer` already distinguishes `built` from `cancelled` for a\ncompleted placement (session 16's finding — completion is an absence and the id\nsurvives it), so the data is there; this is a scope branch and a rollup, not new\nbookkeeping.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `construction {layout_id:\"ly-N\"}` answers for exactly that layout's placements\n  and no others, proven with TWO layouts in flight on one map at overlapping\n  times — one finished, one still awaiting materials.\n- Its rollup distinguishes the states a caller acts on — awaiting-materials,\n  ready, in-progress, blocked, built, cancelled — and a fully built layout is\n  distinguishable from a fully cancelled one, which is the case a rect-scoped\n  read cannot answer at all because both are empty ground.\n- `rect_source` (or its equivalent) names `layout` when a layout scope was used,\n  so the answer says which question it answered.\n- An unknown `layout_id` is a refusal naming the ids this session knows, not a\n  silent fall-through to whole-map — matching the `placement_id` branch, which\n  already refuses with a good sentence about session-scoped ids.\n- A `layout_id` passed together with `rect`/`id`/`placement_id` is a refusal, not\n  a precedence rule.\n- Zero red errors; the layout read does not re-enter a lazy getter per placement.","files":null}]}